================================================================================
Reference material
================================================================================

Neither of these is redistributed in this repository. `reference/` is git-
ignored and must be cloned separately; nothing in the build depends on it.

Fire Dynamics Simulator (FDS)
  Developed by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).
  Software developed by NIST employees is not subject to copyright protection
  within the United States and is in the public domain.

  Where meteor-cfd adapts an approach from FDS, the file in question says so in
  its header. NIST is acknowledged as the source of that material. NIST does
  not endorse meteor-cfd, and provides the FDS software "AS IS" with no
  warranty of any kind.

  Two uses are worth naming because they are data rather than approach. The 168
  WSGG coefficients of `rust/src/wsgg.rs` and `rust/cuda/wsgg.cu` are
  transcribed from `reference/fds/Source/radi.f90`, and the 108 reference total
  emissivities of `RADCAL_EPS` were produced by compiling
  `reference/fds/Source/rcal.f90` - NIST's own RADCAL, NIST TN 1402 - unmodified,
  with the standalone driver in `tools/radcal_emissivity/`. Both are stated in
  the files' own headers and in `rust/PROVENANCE.md`. Neither puts FDS source in
  this repository or in the build.

PyFR
  Copyright (c) Imperial College London and the PyFR authors.
  BSD 3-Clause License.

  Consulted once, as a permissively licensed cross-check while implementing the
  Gmsh `.msh` 4.1 reader (`rust/src/io/msh.rs`): the Gmsh format is published in
  the Gmsh manual and is not copyrightable, and PyFR's own BSD-3 reader was used
  to confirm the interpretation of blocked element ordering. No PyFR source is
  copied into this repository. See `rust/PROVENANCE.md`, "Permissive references".

OpenVDB / NanoVDB
  Copyright (c) DreamWorks Animation LLC and Contributors to the OpenVDB
  Project. Apache License 2.0.

  The `.vdb` archive format has no published byte-layout document, so the
  Apache-2.0 implementation is the only authority for it. It was read - at the
  `v13.0.0` tag, file by file, cited inline in `rust/src/io/vdb.rs` - to write
  an INDEPENDENT writer for that format, first at `fp32` and, for SPEC-LIT
  S45, for the `_HalfFloat` save preference as well. The NanoVDB layout in
  `rust/src/io/nvdb.rs` comes from the Apache-2.0 PNanoVDB/NanoVDB headers the
  same way. No OpenVDB source is copied into this repository and meteor-cfd
  links against no part of it. See `rust/PROVENANCE.md`, "Permissive
  references".

